# TSX single-export folders

Every React component lives in its **own folder**. The `.tsx` file exports **one** thing (the component). Types, hooks, utils, and constants are sibling files in that folder. Sub-components follow the same rule in nested folders or sibling `.tsx` files under the parent folder.

## Layout

```text
button/
  button.tsx              # export function Button only
  button.types.ts         # ButtonProps, ButtonVariant, …
  button.constants.ts     # maps, class tables, magic values
  button.hooks.ts         # optional local hooks
  button.utils.ts         # optional pure helpers
  index.ts                # re-export component + public types
```

Sub-component example:

```text
auth-gate/
  auth-gate.tsx
  auth-gate.types.ts
  clerk-auth-gate.tsx     # one export: ClerkAuthGate
  index.ts
```

## Rules

- **One public runtime export per `.tsx`** — the component (named export preferred).
- **No `export type` / `export const` / helpers in the same `.tsx`** — move to `*.types.ts`, `*.constants.ts`, `*.hooks.ts`, `*.utils.ts`.
- **Import types into the component** from `./name.types.js` (keep `.js` extensions).
- **`index.ts`** re-exports the component and any public types consumers need.
- **Sub-components** get their own `.tsx` (and sibling files if they have types/constants). Do not define a second component in the parent `.tsx`.

## Exceptions

- `*.stories.tsx` — Storybook story exports
- `*.test.tsx` — test suites
- `app/routes/**`, `app/root.tsx` — React Router route module API
- `index.ts` barrels

## ❌ Bad

```tsx
// button.tsx
export type ButtonProps = { … };
export function Button() { … }
const variantClass = { … }; // constants belong in button.constants.ts
```

## ✅ Good

```tsx
// button.types.ts
export type ButtonProps = { … };

// button.constants.ts
export const variantClass = { … };

// button.tsx
import type { ButtonProps } from "./button.types.js";
import { variantClass } from "./button.constants.js";
export function Button(props: ButtonProps) { … }
```
